---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RotatePdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/RotatePdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="rotate-pdf">
  <RotatePdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to rotate PDF pages</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above and wait for the thumbnail grid to appear.</li>
        <li>
          Select nothing to rotate the whole document, or click the pages you want. The quick-select
          buttons select all, odd, even or inverted, and the range box takes input such as <code class="font-mono">2, 5-9, 20-</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Use <strong>90° left</strong>, <strong>90° right</strong> or <strong>180°</strong>. Each
          thumbnail also has two rotate buttons for a single page.
        </li>
        <li>Check the previews, then press <strong>Apply and download</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The grid shows pages as a PDF reader shows them, so the saved file matches the preview.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>PDF rotation is metadata</h2>
      <p>
        Each PDF page has a <code class="font-mono">/Rotate</code> entry of 0, 90, 180 or 270 degrees
        that tells the viewer how to turn the page before it shows it. This tool changes only that
        number. The file size stays the same, images are not re-compressed, and text stays selectable in
        the new orientation.
      </p>
      <p>
        A scanner can store a landscape page as a portrait image with a 90-degree flag. Some tools show
        the raw image and others apply the flag. This tool renders pages the way a viewer does, so the
        previews match any standards-compliant PDF reader.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>PDF rotation reference</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[520px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Problem</th>
              <th scope="col">Fix</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Text reads bottom-to-top</td>
              <td>Rotate 90° right</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Text reads top-to-bottom</td>
              <td>Rotate 90° left</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Page is upside down</td>
              <td>Rotate 180°</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Only the backs of a duplex scan are upside down</td>
              <td>Select <strong>Even</strong>, then rotate 180°</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why rotating in a viewer is not enough</h2>
      <p>
        Most PDF readers have a rotate button in the toolbar, but it changes only the current view. When
        you close the file, the sideways page is back. Some readers save the rotation only in a paid
        version. This tool writes the rotation into the file, so the page prints the right way round and
        looks correct for each person you send it to.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
